<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=de-humani-corporis-fabrica">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">De humani corporis fabrica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as De Humani Corporis Fabrica Libri Septem, On the Fabric of the Human Body</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">De humani corporis fabrica is a set of seven books on human anatomy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-vesalius">Andreas Vesalius</a>, based on his lectures at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and printed by Johannes Oporinus in Basel in 1543. Its more than 250 woodcut illustrations, generally attributed to the studio of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, surpassed those of earlier anatomical atlases. Working from human bodies rather than the animal cadavers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> had used, Vesalius corrected about 300 of Galen's errors, though he kept some, such as different kinds of blood in veins and arteries. More than 700 copies of the 1543 and 1555 editions survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/de-humani-corporis-fabrica.jpg" alt="De humani corporis fabrica" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vesalius_Fabrica_fronticepiece.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1543</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Padua and Basel</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/andreas-vesalius">Andreas Vesalius</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The more than 250 illustrations are generally attributed by modern scholars to the 'studio of Titian' rather than to Johannes Stephanus of Calcar, who drew for Vesalius's earlier tracts.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galenic medicine<span>162 CE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing press<span>c. 1440</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/de-motu-cordis">Circulation of the blood<span>1628</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=de-humani-corporis-fabrica">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
